 its absolute-value derivative. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( dual ), intent(in) :: a Input dual operand; its value and derivative participate in the documented operation. real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: b Input scalar operand or parameter used by the documented operation. Return Value type( dual ) Computed dual-number result containing a value and propagated derivative. private elemental function real_sign_ad(a, b) result(c) Apply AD b's sign to a constant magnitude, returning derivative zero. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=real64), intent(in) :: a Input scalar operand or parameter used by the documented operation. type( dual ), intent(in) :: b Input dual operand; its value and derivative participate in the documented operation. Return Value type( dual ) Computed dual-number result containing a value and propagated derivative. public        interface sin Sine, with arguments in radians. private elemental function ad_sin(a) result(c) Propagate cos(a)*da. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( dual ), intent(in) :: a Input dual operand; its value and derivative participate in the documented operation. Return Value type( dual ) Computed dual-number result containing a value and propagated derivative. public        interface sinh Hyperbolic sine. private elemental function ad_sinh(a) result(c) Propagate cosh(a)*da. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( dual ), intent(in) :: a Input dual operand; its value and derivative participate in the documented operation. Return Value type( dual ) Computed dual-number result containing a value and propagated derivative. public        interface sqrt Square root for nonnegative values; its active derivative is singular at zero. private elemental function ad_sqrt(a) result(c) Propagate da/(2*sqrt(a)); inactive seeds remain zero at a = 0. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( dual ), intent(in) :: a Input dual operand; its value and derivative participate in the documented operation. Return Value type( dual ) Computed dual-number result containing a value and propagated derivative. public        interface tan Tangent, with arguments in radians and away from its poles. private elemental function ad_tan(a) result(c) Propagate da/cos(a)**2. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( dual ), intent(in) :: a Input dual operand; its value and derivative participate in the documented operation. Return Value type( dual ) Computed dual-number result containing a value and propagated derivative. public        interface tanh Hyperbolic tangent. private elemental function ad_tanh(a) result(c) Propagate da/cosh(a)**2 using exp(-abs(a)) to avoid hyperbolic overflow. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( dual ), intent(in) :: a Input dual operand; its value and derivative participate in the documented operation. Return Value type( dual ) Computed dual-number result containing a value and propagated derivative. Derived Types type, public :: dual A value and its directional derivative. Seed dx with one for an\nindependent variable and zero for constants. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=real64), public :: dx Directional derivative with respect to the current seed. real(kind=real64), public :: x Function or independent-variable value.","tags":"","url":"module\\autodiff_dual_numbers.html"},{"title":"autodiff_gradient.f90 – AUTODIFF","text":"Source Code module autodiff_gradient !! Forward-mode evaluation of scalar-function gradients. use iso_fortran_env use autodiff_dual_numbers use autodiff_interfaces use autodiff_workspace implicit none contains subroutine gradient ( fcn , x , g , work , args ) !! Compute a scalar function's gradient by seeding one input direction !! at a time. procedure ( scalar_function ) :: fcn !! User function whose result carries the value and seeded derivative. real ( real64 ), intent ( in ), dimension (:) :: x !! Point at which the gradient is evaluated. real ( real64 ), intent ( out ), dimension (:) :: g !! Gradient output, with one component per input; !! g_i = \\partial f/\\partial x_i. class ( workspace ), intent ( inout ), target , optional :: work !! An optional workspace to use in order to avoid internal memory !! allocations.  If not sized properly, this routine will grow !! the workspace to suit its needs. class ( * ), intent ( in ), optional :: args !! Optional caller-owned context passed to the procedure. type ( dual ), allocatable , target , dimension (:) :: x_buffer type ( dual ), pointer , dimension (:) :: xd type ( dual ) :: fd integer :: i , j , n n = size ( x ) if ( present ( work )) then if (. not . work % validate_size ( n , 1 )) call work % resize ( n , 1 ) xd => work % xd ( 1 : n ) else allocate ( x_buffer ( n )) xd => x_buffer end if do j = 1 , n xd ( j )% x = x ( j ) xd ( j )% dx = 0.0d0 end do do i = 1 , n xd ( i )% dx = 1.0d0 fd = fcn ( xd , args = args ) g ( i ) = fd % dx xd ( i )% dx = 0.0d0 end do end subroutine end module","tags":"","url":"sourcefile\\autodiff_gradient.f90.html"},{"title":"autodiff_hyperdual_workspace.f90 – AUTODIFF","text":"Source Code module autodiff_hyperdual_workspace !! Reusable storage for hyper-dual-valued inputs. use iso_fortran_env use autodiff_hyperdual_numbers implicit none private public :: hyperdual_workspace type :: hyperdual_workspace !! Allocatable temporary arrays used during second-order automatic !! differentiation. type ( hyperdual ), allocatable , dimension (:,:) :: xd_pool !! Per-thread hyper-dual-valued input buffers, one column per !! thread.  Serial evaluations use the first column. contains procedure , public :: resize => hwrk_resize !! Allocates or grows the workspace buffers. procedure , public :: validate_size => hwrk_validate_size !! Validates the size of the workspace. end type contains function hwrk_validate_size ( this , xd_size , nthreads ) result ( rst ) !! Validates that the workspace arrays are allocated and at least !! the size requested. class ( hyperdual_workspace ), intent ( in ) :: this !! The [[hyperdual_workspace]] object. integer , intent ( in ) :: xd_size !! The desired minimum number of input elements per thread. integer , intent ( in ), optional :: nthreads !! The desired number of per-thread buffer columns.  If omitted, !! one column is required. logical :: rst !! True if all buffers meet the minimum required size; else, !! false. integer :: nt nt = 1 if ( present ( nthreads )) nt = max ( 1 , nthreads ) rst = . false . if (. not . allocated ( this % xd_pool )) return rst = size ( this % xd_pool , 1 ) >= xd_size . and . & size ( this % xd_pool , 2 ) >= nt end function subroutine hwrk_resize ( work , n , nthreads ) !! Allocates or grows the workspace buffers to at least the requested !! dimensions.  Buffers are never shrunk.  When growth is required, a !! dimension is sized to the larger of the request and twice its !! previous size.  Buffer contents are not preserved on growth. class ( hyperdual_workspace ), intent ( inout ) :: work !! Workspace whose input buffers are managed. integer , intent ( in ) :: n !! Required number of input elements per thread. integer , intent ( in ), optional :: nthreads !! Required number of per-thread buffer columns.  If omitted, !! one column is required. integer :: nt , rr , cc nt = 1 if ( present ( nthreads )) nt = max ( 1 , nthreads ) if ( allocated ( work % xd_pool )) then if ( size ( work % xd_pool , 1 ) >= n . and . & size ( work % xd_pool , 2 ) >= nt ) return rr = size ( work % xd_pool , 1 ) cc = size ( work % xd_pool , 2 ) if ( rr < n ) rr = max ( n , 2 * rr ) if ( cc < nt ) cc = max ( nt , 2 * cc ) deallocate ( work % xd_pool ) else rr = n cc = nt end if allocate ( work % xd_pool ( rr , cc )) end subroutine end module","tags":"","url":"sourcefile\\autodiff_hyperdual_workspace.f90.html"},{"title":"autodiff_hessian.f90 – AUTODIFF","text":"Source Code module autodiff_hessian !! Second-order forward-mode evaluation of scalar-function gradients and Hessians. use iso_fortran_env use autodiff_hyperdual_numbers use autodiff_interfaces use autodiff_hyperdual_workspace implicit none private public :: hessian public :: hvp contains ! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ subroutine hessian ( fcn , x , H , g , f , work , args , nthreads ) !! Compute a scalar function's Hessian, and optionally its gradient and !! value, by seeding hyper-dual inputs.  The Hessian is symmetric and !! requires n(n+1)/2 evaluations of `fcn`; the gradient and value !! are taken from the diagonal evaluations at no additional cost. !! !! When 'nthreads' is greater than one and the library is built with !! OpenMP, the Hessian rows are evaluated in parallel.  In this case, !! the user function must be thread-safe and must not modify shared !! state through 'args' without synchronization. !$ use omp_lib, only : omp_get_thread_num procedure ( hyperdual_function ) :: fcn !! User function whose result carries the value and derivatives. real ( real64 ), intent ( in ), dimension (:) :: x !! Point at which the Hessian is evaluated. real ( real64 ), intent ( out ), dimension (:,:) :: H !! Hessian output, H_{ij} = \\partial&#94;2 f / \\partial x_i \\partial x_j. real ( real64 ), intent ( out ), optional , dimension (:) :: g !! Optional gradient output, g_i = \\partial f/\\partial x_i. real ( real64 ), intent ( out ), optional :: f !! Optional function value at `x`. class ( hyperdual_workspace ), intent ( inout ), target , optional :: work !! An optional workspace to use in order to avoid internal memory !! allocations.  If not sized properly, this routine will grow !! the workspace to suit its needs. class ( * ), intent ( in ), optional :: args !! Optional caller-owned context passed to the function. integer , intent ( in ), optional :: nthreads !! The maximum number of threads to use when evaluating the !! Hessian rows.  The default is 1 (serial). type ( hyperdual ), allocatable , target , dimension (:,:) :: x_buffer type ( hyperdual ), pointer , dimension (:,:) :: xd type ( hyperdual ) :: fd integer :: i , n , nt , tid n = size ( x ) nt = 1 if ( present ( nthreads )) nt = max ( 1 , nthreads ) if ( present ( work )) then if (. not . work % validate_size ( n , nt )) call work % resize ( n , nt ) xd => work % xd_pool ( 1 : n , 1 : nt ) else allocate ( x_buffer ( n , nt )) xd => x_buffer end if if ( n == 0 ) then if ( present ( f )) then fd = fcn ( xd (:, 1 ), args = args ) f = fd % x end if return end if if ( nt == 1 ) then ! Run serial, but do not engage with OpenMP call init_inputs ( x , xd (:, 1 )) do i = 1 , n call hessian_row ( fcn , xd (:, 1 ), i , H , g , f , args ) end do else ! Utilize OpenMP.  Row i requires n - i + 1 evaluations, so rows ! are dealt cyclically to balance the triangular workload. !$omp parallel num_threads(nt) default(shared) private(i, tid) tid = 1 !$      tid = omp_get_thread_num() + 1 ! Each thread owns one column of the buffer.  Inputs are set once ! per thread, and only the seeded directions are toggled per entry. call init_inputs ( x , xd (:, tid )) !$omp do schedule(static, 1) do i = 1 , n call hessian_row ( fcn , xd (:, tid ), i , H , g , f , args ) end do !$omp end do !$omp end parallel end if end subroutine ! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ subroutine hvp ( fcn , x , v , Hv , work , args ) !! Evaluate the product of a scalar function's Hessian and a vector !! without forming the Hessian.  Each evaluation seeds the first !! derivative direction with `v` and the second with a unit vector, !! so the mixed derivative yields one component of Hv.  This !! requires `size(x)` evaluations of `fcn`. procedure ( hyperdual_function ) :: fcn !! User function whose result carries the value and derivatives. real ( real64 ), intent ( in ), dimension (:) :: x !! Point at which the Hessian is evaluated. real ( real64 ), intent ( in ), dimension (:) :: v !! Direction vector with one component per input. real ( real64 ), intent ( out ), dimension (:) :: Hv !! Product of the Hessian and v, satisfying Hv = H_f(x)v. class ( hyperdual_workspace ), intent ( inout ), target , optional :: work !! An optional workspace to use in order to avoid internal memory !! allocations.  If not sized properly, this routine will grow !! the workspace to suit its needs. class ( * ), intent ( in ), optional :: args !! Optional caller-owned context passed to the function. type ( hyperdual ), allocatable , target , dimension (:,:) :: x_buffer type ( hyperdual ), pointer , dimension (:,:) :: xd type ( hyperdual ) :: fd integer :: i , n n = size ( x ) if ( present ( work )) then if (. not . work % validate_size ( n )) call work % resize ( n ) xd => work % xd_pool ( 1 : n , 1 : 1 ) else allocate ( x_buffer ( n , 1 )) xd => x_buffer end if call init_inputs ( x , xd (:, 1 )) do i = 1 , n xd ( i , 1 )% dx = v ( i ) end do do i = 1 , n xd ( i , 1 )% dy = 1.0d0 fd = fcn ( xd (:, 1 ), args = args ) Hv ( i ) = fd % dxy xd ( i , 1 )% dy = 0.0d0 end do end subroutine ! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ subroutine init_inputs ( x , xd ) !! Copies the evaluation point into a hyper-dual buffer and clears !! all derivative seeds. real ( real64 ), intent ( in ), dimension (:) :: x !! Point at which derivatives are evaluated. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( inout ), dimension (:) :: xd !! Hyper-dual input buffer with at least size(x) elements. integer :: k do k = 1 , size ( x ) xd ( k ) = x ( k ) end do end subroutine ! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ subroutine hessian_row ( fcn , xd , i , H , g , f , args ) !! Evaluates the upper-triangular entries of row 'i' of the Hessian !! and mirrors them into column 'i'.  The diagonal evaluation also !! supplies the gradient component and, for the first row, the value. !! The seeds in 'xd' are restored to zero on return. procedure ( hyperdual_function ) :: fcn !! User function whose result carries the value and derivatives. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( inout ), dimension (:) :: xd !! Hyper-dual input buffer with values set and seeds cleared. integer , intent ( in ) :: i !! Row index. real ( real64 ), intent ( inout ), dimension (:,:) :: H !! Hessian output. real ( real64 ), intent ( inout ), optional , dimension (:) :: g !! Optional gradient output. real ( real64 ), intent ( inout ), optional :: f !! Optional function value output. class ( * ), intent ( in ), optional :: args !! Optional caller-owned context passed to the function. type ( hyperdual ) :: fd integer :: j xd ( i )% dx = 1.0d0 do j = i , size ( xd ) xd ( j )% dy = 1.0d0 fd = fcn ( xd , args = args ) H ( i , j ) = fd % dxy H ( j , i ) = fd % dxy if ( j == i ) then if ( present ( g )) g ( i ) = fd % dx if ( i == 1 . and . present ( f )) f = fd % x end if xd ( j )% dy = 0.0d0 end do xd ( i )% dx = 0.0d0 end subroutine ! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ end module","tags":"","url":"sourcefile\\autodiff_hessian.f90.html"},{"title":"autodiff_jvp.f90 – AUTODIFF","text":"Source Code module autodiff_jvp !! Forward-mode evaluation of Jacobian-vector products. use iso_fortran_env use autodiff_dual_numbers use autodiff_interfaces use autodiff_workspace contains subroutine jvp ( fcn , x , v , Jv , work , args ) !! Evaluate the directional derivative of a vector function without !! forming its Jacobian. procedure ( vector_function ) :: fcn !! User procedure that evaluates the vector-valued function. real ( real64 ), intent ( in ) :: x (:) !! Input point at which the directional derivative is evaluated. real ( real64 ), intent ( in ), dimension (:) :: v !! Direction vector, with one component per input; its entries seed !! each dual input. real ( real64 ), intent ( out ), dimension (:) :: Jv !! Product of the Jacobian and v, satisfying Jv = J_f(x)v. class ( workspace ), intent ( inout ), target , optional :: work !! An optional workspace to use in order to avoid internal memory !! allocations.  If not sized properly, this routine will grow !! the workspace to suit its needs. class ( * ), intent ( in ), optional :: args !! Optional caller-owned context passed to the procedure. type ( dual ), allocatable , target , dimension (:) :: x_buffer , f_buffer type ( dual ), pointer , dimension (:) :: xd , fd integer :: i , n , m n = size ( x ) m = size ( Jv ) if ( present ( work )) then if (. not . work % validate_size ( n , m )) call work % resize ( n , m ) xd => work % xd ( 1 : n ) fd => work % fd ( 1 : m ) else allocate ( x_buffer ( n ), f_buffer ( m )) xd => x_buffer fd => f_buffer end if do concurrent ( i = 1 : n ) xd ( i )% x = x ( i ) xd ( i )% dx = v ( i ) end do call fcn ( xd , fd , args = args ) do i = 1 , m Jv ( i ) = fd ( i )% dx end do end subroutine end module","tags":"","url":"sourcefile\\autodiff_jvp.f90.html"},{"title":"autodiff.f90 – AUTODIFF","text":"Source Code module autodiff use autodiff_dual_numbers use autodiff_interfaces use autodiff_workspace use autodiff_gradient use autodiff_jacobian use autodiff_jvp use autodiff_hyperdual_numbers use autodiff_hyperdual_workspace use autodiff_hessian end module","tags":"","url":"sourcefile\\autodiff.f90.html"},{"title":"autodiff_workspace.f90 – AUTODIFF","text":"Source Code module autodiff_workspace !! Reusable storage for dual-valued inputs and outputs. use iso_fortran_env use autodiff_dual_numbers implicit none private public :: workspace type :: workspace !! Allocatable temporary arrays used during automatic differentiation. type ( dual ), allocatable , dimension (:) :: xd !! Dual-valued input buffer. type ( dual ), allocatable , dimension (:) :: fd !! Dual-valued function output buffer. type ( dual ), allocatable , dimension (:,:) :: xd_pool !! Per-thread dual-valued input buffers, one column per thread. type ( dual ), allocatable , dimension (:,:) :: fd_pool !! Per-thread dual-valued function output buffers, one column per thread. contains procedure , public :: resize => wrk_resize !! Allocates or grows the workspace buffers. procedure , public :: validate_size => wrk_validate_size !! Validates the size of the workspace. end type contains function wrk_validate_size ( this , xd_size , fd_size , nthreads ) result ( rst ) !! Validates that the workspace arrays are allocated and at least !! the size requested. class ( workspace ), intent ( in ) :: this !! The [[workspace]] object. integer , intent ( in ) :: xd_size !! The desired minimum size of 'xd'. integer , intent ( in ) :: fd_size !! The desired minimum size of 'fd'. integer , intent ( in ), optional :: nthreads !! The desired number of per-thread buffer columns.  If omitted, !! one column is required. logical :: rst !! True if all buffers meet the minimum required size; else, !! false. integer :: nt nt = 1 if ( present ( nthreads )) nt = max ( 1 , nthreads ) rst = . false . if (. not . allocated ( this % xd )) return if (. not . allocated ( this % fd )) return if (. not . allocated ( this % xd_pool )) return if (. not . allocated ( this % fd_pool )) return rst = size ( this % xd ) >= xd_size . and . size ( this % fd ) >= fd_size if (. not . rst ) return rst = size ( this % xd_pool , 1 ) >= xd_size . and . & size ( this % xd_pool , 2 ) >= nt . and . & size ( this % fd_pool , 1 ) >= fd_size . and . & size ( this % fd_pool , 2 ) >= nt end function subroutine wrk_resize ( work , n , m , nthreads ) !! Allocates or grows the workspace buffers to at least the requested !! dimensions.  Buffers are never shrunk.  When growth is required, a !! buffer is sized to the larger of the request and twice its !! previous size.  Buffer contents are not preserved on growth. class ( workspace ), intent ( inout ) :: work !! Workspace whose input and output buffers are managed. integer , intent ( in ) :: n !! Required number of input elements. integer , intent ( in ) :: m !! Required number of output elements. integer , intent ( in ), optional :: nthreads !! Required number of per-thread buffer columns.  If omitted, !! one column is required. integer :: nt nt = 1 if ( present ( nthreads )) nt = max ( 1 , nthreads ) call grow_vector ( work % xd , n ) call grow_vector ( work % fd , m ) call grow_matrix ( work % xd_pool , n , nt ) call grow_matrix ( work % fd_pool , m , nt ) end subroutine subroutine grow_vector ( v , n ) !! Grows an allocatable vector to at least 'n' elements. type ( dual ), allocatable , intent ( inout ) :: v (:) !! The vector to grow. integer , intent ( in ) :: n !! The required number of elements. integer :: nn if ( allocated ( v )) then if ( size ( v ) >= n ) return nn = max ( n , 2 * size ( v )) deallocate ( v ) else nn = n end if allocate ( v ( nn )) end subroutine subroutine grow_matrix ( a , nr , nc ) !! Grows an allocatable matrix to at least 'nr' rows and 'nc' columns. type ( dual ), allocatable , intent ( inout ) :: a (:,:) !! The matrix to grow. integer , intent ( in ) :: nr !! The required number of rows. integer , intent ( in ) :: nc !! The required number of columns. integer :: rr , cc if ( allocated ( a )) then if ( size ( a , 1 ) >= nr . and . size ( a , 2 ) >= nc ) return rr = size ( a , 1 ) cc = size ( a , 2 ) if ( rr < nr ) rr = max ( nr , 2 * rr ) if ( cc < nc ) cc = max ( nc , 2 * cc ) deallocate ( a ) else rr = nr cc = nc end if allocate ( a ( rr , cc )) end subroutine end module","tags":"","url":"sourcefile\\autodiff_workspace.f90.html"},{"title":"autodiff_jacobian.f90 – AUTODIFF","text":"Source Code module autodiff_jacobian !! Forward-mode evaluation of vector-function Jacobian matrices. use iso_fortran_env use autodiff_dual_numbers use autodiff_interfaces use autodiff_workspace implicit none contains subroutine jacobian ( fcn , x , J , work , args , nthreads ) !! Compute a vector function's Jacobian by seeding each input basis !! direction. !! !! When 'nthreads' is greater than one and the library is built with !! OpenMP, the Jacobian columns are evaluated in parallel.  In this !! case, the user procedure must be thread-safe and must not modify !! shared state through 'args' without synchronization. !$ use omp_lib, only : omp_get_thread_num procedure ( vector_function ) :: fcn !! User procedure that evaluates the vector-valued function. real ( real64 ), intent ( in ), dimension (:) :: x !! Input point at which the Jacobian is evaluated. real ( real64 ), intent ( out ), dimension (:,:) :: J !! Jacobian output with shape (number of outputs, number of inputs); !! J_{ik} = \\partial f_i/\\partial x_k. class ( workspace ), intent ( inout ), target , optional :: work !! An optional workspace to use in order to avoid internal memory !! allocations.  If not sized properly, this routine will grow !! the workspace to suit its needs. class ( * ), intent ( in ), optional :: args !! Optional caller-owned context passed to the procedure. integer , intent ( in ), optional :: nthreads !! The maximum number of threads to use when evaluating the !! Jacobian columns.  The default is 1 (serial). type ( dual ), allocatable , target , dimension (:,:) :: x_buffer , f_buffer type ( dual ), pointer , dimension (:,:) :: xd , fd integer :: i , k , m , n , nt , tid n = size ( x ) m = size ( J , 1 ) nt = 1 if ( present ( nthreads )) nt = max ( 1 , nthreads ) if ( present ( work )) then if (. not . work % validate_size ( n , m , nt )) call work % resize ( n , m , nt ) xd => work % xd_pool ( 1 : n , 1 : nt ) fd => work % fd_pool ( 1 : m , 1 : nt ) else allocate ( x_buffer ( n , nt ), f_buffer ( m , nt )) xd => x_buffer fd => f_buffer end if if ( nt == 1 ) then ! Run serial, but do not engage with OpenMP ! Each thread owns one column of the buffers.  Inputs are set once ! per thread, and only the seeded direction is toggled per column. do i = 1 , n xd ( i , 1 )% x = x ( i ) xd ( i , 1 )% dx = 0.0d0 end do do k = 1 , n xd ( k , 1 )% dx = 1.0d0 call fcn ( xd (:, 1 ), fd (:, 1 ), args = args ) do i = 1 , m J ( i , k ) = fd ( i , 1 )% dx end do xd ( k , 1 )% dx = 0.0d0 end do else ! Utilize OpenMP !$omp parallel num_threads(nt) default(shared) private(i, k, tid) tid = 1 !$      tid = omp_get_thread_num() + 1 ! Each thread owns one column of the buffers.  Inputs are set once ! per thread, and only the seeded direction is toggled per column. do i = 1 , n xd ( i , tid )% x = x ( i ) xd ( i , tid )% dx = 0.0d0 end do !$omp do schedule(static) do k = 1 , n xd ( k , tid )% dx = 1.0d0 call fcn ( xd (:, tid ), fd (:, tid ), args = args ) do i = 1 , m J ( i , k ) = fd ( i , tid )% dx end do xd ( k , tid )% dx = 0.0d0 end do !$omp end do !$omp end parallel end if end subroutine end module","tags":"","url":"sourcefile\\autodiff_jacobian.f90.html"},{"title":"autodiff_hyperdual_numbers.f90 – AUTODIFF","text":"Source Code module autodiff_hyperdual_numbers !! Second-order forward-mode hyper-dual numbers and overloaded operations. use iso_fortran_env implicit none private public :: hyperdual !! Hyper-dual number type carrying a value, two first derivatives, and a mixed second derivative. public :: assignment ( = ) !! Assignment conversions between hyper-dual numbers and real or integer scalars. public :: operator ( + ), operator ( - ), operator ( * ), operator ( / ), operator ( ** ) !! Arithmetic operators propagating first and mixed second derivatives. public :: operator ( == ), operator ( /= ), operator ( < ), operator ( <= ) !! Comparisons use values only and ignore derivative seeds. public :: operator ( > ), operator ( >= ) !! Remaining value-only ordering comparisons. public :: abs , sqrt , exp , log , log10 , sin , cos , tan , asin , acos , atan , atan2 public :: sinh , cosh , tanh , asinh , acosh , atanh , erf , erfc , min , max , sign !! Overloaded real-valued intrinsic functions with second-order derivative propagation. type hyperdual !! A value, two directional derivatives, and their mixed second derivative. !! Seed `dx` and `dy` with one for each independent variable under study !! and zero otherwise. real ( real64 ) :: x !! Function or independent-variable value. real ( real64 ) :: dx !! First derivative with respect to the first seed. real ( real64 ) :: dy !! First derivative with respect to the second seed. real ( real64 ) :: dxy !! Mixed second derivative with respect to both seeds. end type interface assignment ( = ) !! Copy hyper-dual data, assign a constant (clearing derivatives), or extract a real value. module procedure :: hd_assign_hd module procedure :: hd_assign_real module procedure :: hd_assign_integer module procedure :: real_assign_hd end interface interface operator ( + ) !! Addition, including constant operands in either order. module procedure :: hd_add , hd_add_real , real_add_hd module procedure :: hd_add_integer , integer_add_hd end interface interface operator ( - ) !! Subtraction and unary negation, including constant operands. module procedure :: hd_subtract , hd_subtract_real , real_subtract_hd module procedure :: hd_subtract_integer , integer_subtract_hd , hd_negative end interface interface operator ( * ) !! Multiplication using the product rule to second order. module procedure :: hd_multiply , hd_multiply_real , real_multiply_hd module procedure :: hd_multiply_integer , integer_multiply_hd end interface interface operator ( / ) !! Division; the denominator must be nonzero. module procedure :: hd_divide , hd_divide_real , real_divide_hd module procedure :: hd_divide_integer , integer_divide_hd end interface interface operator ( ** ) !! Powers with constant or hyper-dual exponents.  A real or hyper-dual base !! raised to a non-integer power must be positive. module procedure :: hd_pow_integer , hd_pow_real , hd_pow_hd module procedure :: real_pow_hd end interface interface operator ( == ) !! Value-only equality. module procedure :: hd_eq , hd_eq_real , real_eq_hd end interface interface operator ( /= ) !! Value-only inequality. module procedure :: hd_ne , hd_ne_real , real_ne_hd end interface interface operator ( < ) !! Value-only less-than comparison. module procedure :: hd_lt , hd_lt_real , real_lt_hd end interface interface operator ( <= ) !! Value-only less-than-or-equal comparison. module procedure :: hd_le , hd_le_real , real_le_hd end interface interface operator ( > ) !! Value-only greater-than comparison. module procedure :: hd_gt , hd_gt_real , real_gt_hd end interface interface operator ( >= ) !! Value-only greater-than-or-equal comparison. module procedure :: hd_ge , hd_ge_real , real_ge_hd end interface interface abs !! Absolute value; the derivative at zero is taken as that of the positive branch. module procedure :: hd_abs end interface interface sqrt !! Square root of a positive value. module procedure :: hd_sqrt end interface interface exp !! Exponential function. module procedure :: hd_exp end interface interface log !! Natural logarithm of a positive value. module procedure :: hd_log end interface interface log10 !! Base-ten logarithm of a positive value. module procedure :: hd_log10 end interface interface sin !! Sine function. module procedure :: hd_sin end interface interface cos !! Cosine function. module procedure :: hd_cos end interface interface tan !! Tangent function. module procedure :: hd_tan end interface interface asin !! Inverse sine function. module procedure :: hd_asin end interface interface acos !! Inverse cosine function. module procedure :: hd_acos end interface interface atan !! One- or two-argument inverse tangent function. module procedure :: hd_atan , hd_atan2 , hd_atan2_real , real_atan2_hd end interface interface atan2 !! Quadrant-aware inverse tangent with ordinate first. module procedure :: hd_atan2 , hd_atan2_real , real_atan2_hd end interface interface sinh !! Hyperbolic sine function. module procedure :: hd_sinh end interface interface cosh !! Hyperbolic cosine function. module procedure :: hd_cosh end interface interface tanh !! Hyperbolic tangent function. module procedure :: hd_tanh end interface interface asinh !! Inverse hyperbolic sine function. module procedure :: hd_asinh end interface interface acosh !! Inverse hyperbolic cosine function. module procedure :: hd_acosh end interface interface atanh !! Inverse hyperbolic tangent function. module procedure :: hd_atanh end interface interface erf !! Error function. module procedure :: hd_erf end interface interface erfc !! Complementary error function. module procedure :: hd_erfc end interface interface min !! Minimum, carrying the selected operand's derivatives; ties choose the first. module procedure :: hd_min , hd_min_real , real_min_hd end interface interface max !! Maximum, carrying the selected operand's derivatives; ties choose the first. module procedure :: hd_max , hd_max_real , real_max_hd end interface interface sign !! Magnitude of the first operand with the sign of the second. module procedure :: hd_sign , hd_sign_real , real_sign_hd end interface contains ! Builds a constant hyper-dual number (all derivatives zero). pure function hd_const ( c ) result ( h ) real ( real64 ), intent ( in ) :: c type ( hyperdual ) :: h h % x = c h % dx = 0.0d0 h % dy = 0.0d0 h % dxy = 0.0d0 end function ! Applies the chain rule for f(a) given f(a.x), f'(a.x), and f''(a.x). pure function hd_compose ( a , f0 , f1 , f2 ) result ( c ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: a real ( real64 ), intent ( in ) :: f0 !! The function value at a%x. real ( real64 ), intent ( in ) :: f1 !! The first derivative of the function at a%x. real ( real64 ), intent ( in ) :: f2 !! The second derivative of the function at a%x. type ( hyperdual ) :: c c % x = f0 c % dx = f1 * a % dx c % dy = f1 * a % dy c % dxy = f1 * a % dxy + f2 * a % dx * a % dy end function pure subroutine hd_assign_hd ( lhs , rhs ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( out ) :: lhs type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: rhs lhs % x = rhs % x lhs % dx = rhs % dx lhs % dy = rhs % dy lhs % dxy = rhs % dxy end subroutine pure subroutine hd_assign_real ( lhs , rhs ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( out ) :: lhs real ( real64 ), intent ( in ) :: rhs lhs = hd_const ( rhs ) end subroutine pure subroutine hd_assign_integer ( lhs , rhs ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( out ) :: lhs integer , intent ( in ) :: rhs lhs = hd_const ( real ( rhs , real64 )) end subroutine pure subroutine real_assign_hd ( lhs , rhs ) real ( real64 ), intent ( out ) :: lhs type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: rhs lhs = rhs % x end subroutine ! Addition and subtraction. pure function hd_add ( a , b ) result ( c ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: a , b type ( hyperdual ) :: c c % x = a % x + b % x c % dx = a % dx + b % dx c % dy = a % dy + b % dy c % dxy = a % dxy + b % dxy end function pure function hd_add_real ( a , b ) result ( c ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: a real ( real64 ), intent ( in ) :: b type ( hyperdual ) :: c c = hd_add ( a , hd_const ( b )) end function pure function real_add_hd ( a , b ) result ( c ) real ( real64 ), intent ( in ) :: a type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: b type ( hyperdual ) :: c c = hd_add ( hd_const ( a ), b ) end function pure function hd_add_integer ( a , b ) result ( c ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: a integer , intent ( in ) :: b type ( hyperdual ) :: c c = hd_add ( a , hd_const ( real ( b , real64 ))) end function pure function integer_add_hd ( a , b ) result ( c ) integer , intent ( in ) :: a type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: b type ( hyperdual ) :: c c = hd_add ( hd_const ( real ( a , real64 )), b ) end function pure function hd_subtract ( a , b ) result ( c ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: a , b type ( hyperdual ) :: c c % x = a % x - b % x c % dx = a % dx - b % dx c % dy = a % dy - b % dy c % dxy = a % dxy - b % dxy end function pure function hd_subtract_real ( a , b ) result ( c ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: a real ( real64 ), intent ( in ) :: b type ( hyperdual ) :: c c = hd_subtract ( a , hd_const ( b )) end function pure function real_subtract_hd ( a , b ) result ( c ) real ( real64 ), intent ( in ) :: a type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: b type ( hyperdual ) :: c c = hd_subtract ( hd_const ( a ), b )